    position_id: "f38ceb6b-1b9c-4acd-9670-dc8af03ad804",
    title: "Payment Operations Associate",
    jd_reference: "BVT/HR/JD/2026/003",
    summary: "Make and verify payments in BlynkEx's 24x7 Payment Operations team: send UPI, IMPS, NEFT and RTGS payouts exactly as instructed, check every incoming client payment with care, and keep a complete record as you go. Full training is provided; the role works rotational shifts, including nights, and what matters most is accuracy and integrity.",
    description: "As a Payment Operations Associate you make outgoing payments and verify incoming ones for your shift, accurately, on time and on a complete record. You work only on approved payment instructions and take anything unusual to your Payment Operations Executive.\n\nAccuracy is the standard in this role, and speed matters only within it: you check every detail before you act, however busy the shift or insistent the request.\n\nWhat you'll do\n• Make UPI, IMPS, NEFT and RTGS payouts accurately and on time, exactly as set out in each approved instruction\n• Verify incoming client payments carefully, and confirm each one only once every check is complete\n• Record each payment's transaction reference (UTR) and confirmation at the moment you make it\n• Reconcile your shift's payments before the shift closes, so it never ends with an unexplained difference\n• Hand over open items to the incoming Associate in writing at the change of shift\n• Report a failed payment, a channel outage or any detail that does not add up to your Payment Operations Executive straight away\n• Keep client and payment details strictly confidential, and follow every security rule for the systems you use\n\nWho you'll work with\nYou'll report to the Payment Operations Executive on your shift, in the Liquidity and Settlements function led by the Head of Liquidity & Settlements, and work most closely with the other Payment Operations Associates and the Operations team.",
    requirements: "• Graduate in any discipline\n• One year or more in payment processing, banking operations, accounts payable or cash handling; strong fresh graduates with proven numerical accuracy may also be considered\n• Working knowledge of UPI, IMPS, NEFT and RTGS, including cut-off timings and what happens when a payment fails\n• Comfortable working accurately and at speed on banking and payment systems\n• Accurate with numbers under time pressure, through a full shift\n• Willing to work rotational shifts, including the night shift\n• Able to clear background and reference verification\n\nGood to have\n• A payment gateway, NBFC or bank back-office payments background\n• Experience of bank reconciliation\n• Awareness of AML red flags in payment flows\n\nYou'll do well here if you\n• Check before you act, every time, however routine the payment\n• Report your own errors straight away, without being asked\n• Stay calm and stick to the process under pressure from a waiting client or colleague\n• Bring complete personal integrity to your work",
    benefits: "What we offer\n• Fixed, transparent pay: ₹13,600 a month (CTC) on the morning shift, the same for every new Payment Operations Associate\n• A shift allowance on top for the evening and night shifts\n• A paid first month of structured training before you handle live payments\n• Paid leave: 12 casual leave and 6 sick leave days a year, plus compensatory off for eligible extra days worked\n• PF and ESI as applicable\n• A clear growth path from Associate to Payment Operations Executive and Settlement Operations Manager, and monthly Employee of the Month recognition\n\nBefore you join\n• Joining dates fall on Mondays, around the 7th, 14th, 21st and 28th of each month\n• Your offer is subject to background and reference verification and a police clearance certificate",
    hr_notes: "Standard terms\n• Fixed CTC ₹13,600 a month on the morning shift, plus the evening/night shift allowance; no negotiation (L8/L7 hiring standard, Oct 2026)\n• Women are rostered on the morning shift only\n• Joining only on the Monday nearest the 7th, 14th, 21st or 28th; the next working day if that Monday is a holiday\n• Police clearance certificate and the background-verification checklist are compulsory before joining\n• Training-month pay, security deposit, probation and notice are set in the offer letter as per the current hiring standard\n\nWhat to test in the interview\n• Credentials: a supervisor asks for their OTP to push a payment through. The right answer is to refuse, whoever asks, and point them to the Payment Operations Executive\n• Incoming payments: a client sends a payment screenshot but nothing shows on the bank statement. The right answer is no confirmation until the credit appears with matching amount, remitter and reference\n• Accuracy exercise: five sample payouts to check against KYC records, one with a wrong IFSC and one with a name mismatch. They should catch both and escalate, not correct them\n• Own errors: they have just paid the wrong beneficiary. The right answer is to tell the Payment Operations Executive at once; a reported error is recoverable, concealment is a conduct matter\n• Night shifts: confirm they can work the rotation, including nights\n\nKeep in mind\n• On a shift with a single Associate, the Payment Operations Executive is the checker; the role is never run unchecked\n• The rule never to hand over a credential or OTP, even to a supervisor, is absolute; it protects the Associate as much as the company, because any misuse is traced to their credential\n\nSource: JD Compendium v4.2, BVT/HR/JD/2026/003",
